course: dictionary
lesson: 7256
title: Boxes, labels and the pigeonhole
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A pigeonhole is one of a set of small open boxes, in a wall or a desk, where letters and papers are put for each person. Context tells you this: teachers' letters go into pigeonholes in the staff room.
explain: As a verb, to pigeonhole someone means to decide too quickly that they belong to one simple type, and that is often unfair. «Don't pigeonhole people» means don't judge them as all the same before you know them.
explain: «With socks of only two colours, 3 socks are always enough to be sure of a matching pair; grown-ups call this the pigeonhole principle.» Swap in your meaning: the socks go into two colour boxes, and with three socks, two must share a box. That is why they match. The word still makes sense.
fact: A pigeonhole is one of many small open boxes in a wall or desk, for letters and papers.
fact: In a staff room, teachers often have a pigeonhole each for their letters.
fact: To pigeonhole someone is to decide too quickly that they belong to one simple type, which is unfair.
fact: Similar words for this unfair boxing-up are label and stereotype.
fact: The pigeonhole principle is a maths idea: if more things than boxes are put in, at least one box must hold two of them.
exercise: «The teacher put the letter in my pigeonhole.» What is a pigeonhole here? | *A small open box for letters | A pigeon's nest in a tree | A hole in the road | A kind of bird food
exercise: «Don't pigeonhole people.» What does pigeonhole mean in this sentence? | *To judge someone too quickly as one simple type | To feed the pigeons | To dig a deep hole | To give someone a prize
exercise: Which word has a similar meaning to pigeonhole, as in putting people in a box? | *Label | Pigeon | Principle | Hole
test: «With socks of only two colours, 3 socks are always enough to be sure of a matching pair.» What does this show? | Socks always come in pairs | *If three things go into two boxes, two must share a box | Every box holds exactly one sock | Two socks are always different colours
test: Which sentence uses pigeonhole as a verb? | Put the socks in the drawer | *They pigeonholed him as the quiet one, without asking him anything | The pigeons flew into the hole | The letter sat in my pigeonhole
exam: Why is it unfair to pigeonhole people? | It means they are good at maths | It means they must wear socks | It is fun to make lists | *It judges people as one simple type without really knowing them
